class:: FBrownianMotion
summary:: Finite-memory control-rate fractional Brownian approximation
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>Processes
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/FractionalNoise, Classes/OUProcess

description::

Fractional Brownian Motion (fBm) generalizes standard Brownian motion by introducing a Hurst exponent, which mathematically defines the autocorrelation of the random steps. Conceptually, this determines the roughness or smoothness of the path. A high Hurst exponent produces persistent, smooth, rolling macro-modulations, while a low exponent produces anti-persistent, jagged, jittery noise. This makes it an incredibly versatile stochastic generator for everything from slow tectonic parameter shifts to erratic, textured noise.

Builds fractional Gaussian-like increments with a finite ARFIMA-style coefficient sequence and integrates them at control rate. Hurst values below 0.5 are anti-persistent, 0.5 is Brownian-like, and values above 0.5 are persistent.

This is a finite-memory control-rate approximation. Its output is nonstationary and unbounded. Unlike link::Classes/BrownNoise::, it exposes Hurst behavior, time scaling, memory size, deterministic seed, and reset.

subsection:: Persistence view

image::fbrownian-paths.png#Finite-memory paths for Hurst = 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8, shown in muted teal, blue, and plum. Each path is vertically normalized for comparing texture: low H reverses direction often, while high H sustains trends.::

classmethods::

method:: kr
argument:: hurst
Persistence exponent, clipped to 0.01 through 0.99.
argument:: timeScale
Nonnegative model-time scale applied to each control duration.
argument:: initial
Initial unbounded state.
argument:: memorySize
Init-rate innovation memory, clipped to 8 through 4096.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Clear memory and restore initial state and seed on a rising edge.
returns:: One control-rate nonstationary process state.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
{ FBrownianMotion.kr.poll }.play
)
::

code::
(
{ SinOsc.ar(FBrownianMotion.kr(0.8, 0.2).fold(0, 1).linexp(0, 1, 80, 3000), 0, 0.08) }.play
)
::

code::
// Tectonic Macro-Modulation
(
{
    var hurst, walk, freq;
    // Adjust the Hurst exponent to move from jittery noise to smooth, rolling landscapes
    hurst = MouseX.kr(0.1, 0.9);
    walk = FBrownianMotion.kr(hurst: hurst);
    freq = walk.linexp(-2, 2, 100, 1000);
    Pan2.ar(SinOsc.ar(freq) * 0.2)
}.play;
)
::
